## 1. slug: how-audit-battery-factory
- title: "How to Audit a Lead-Acid Battery Factory | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "How to audit a lead-acid battery factory for OEM sourcing: certifications, production capability, quality control, testing and export readiness."
- articleTitle: "How to audit a lead-acid battery factory"
- lead: "A factory audit is how you verify that a battery manufacturer can deliver your specification repeatably — not just once. Run these checks before committing volume."
- body:
  - h2 "Certifications" → "Request current certificates, not claims. Chengguang Energy holds IATF 16949, ISO 9001, ISO 45001 and OHSAS 18001."
  - h2 "Production capability" → "Check factory area, number of production lines and daily output against your annual volume. Chengguang Energy operates a 200,000 m² plant with 18 production lines producing 40,000+ batteries a day."
  - h2 "Quality control & testing" → bullet list: "Incoming material inspection", "In-process checks (grid casting, pasting, curing, formation)", "100% end-of-line electrical test", "Batch traceability", "Lab testing for CCA and capacity"
  - h2 "Export readiness" → "Confirm export experience, documentation capability and destination markets. Chengguang Energy exports to 70+ countries."
  - h2 "What to bring home from an audit" → bullet list: "Current certificates", "Sample of a recent production run", "A specification sheet for your reference model", "Agreed commercial terms"
- internal: link to /knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/ and /factory/

## 2. slug: oem-battery-rfq-guide
- title: "OEM Battery RFQ Guide: Write an Effective Request for Quotation | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "How to write an OEM battery RFQ that gets accurate quotes: specification fields, volume, packaging, certification and delivery requirements."
- articleTitle: "OEM battery RFQ guide"
- lead: "A complete RFQ gets you comparable quotes; a vague one gets you a range so wide it is useless. Include these fields."
- body:
  - h2 "The specification block" → bullet: "Battery type (SLI, AGM, EFB, heavy-duty)", "Standard family (JIS, DIN, BCI, AS, SA)", "Reference model (e.g. 65D26)", "Voltage, capacity (Ah), CCA", "Case size, terminal type, polarity"
  - h2 "The volume block" → bullet: "Annual volume", "First order quantity", "Forecast by model if mixed", "Target market (affects standard and certification)"
  - h2 "The branding block" → bullet: "Private label or OEM", "Label artwork and packaging", "Pallet configuration", "Multi-language documentation"
  - h2 "The commercial block" → bullet: "Target price (optional)", "Incoterms", "Payment terms", "Delivery schedule"
- internal: link to /contact/ (RFQ form) and /knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/

## 3. slug: oem-battery-moq-lead-time
- title: "OEM Battery MOQ & Lead Time Explained | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "What MOQ and lead time mean for OEM battery orders, and how they are set — with Chengguang Energy's reference terms."
- articleTitle: "OEM battery MOQ & lead time"
- lead: "MOQ and lead time are set by production economics and container logistics, not by negotiation alone. Here is how they work."
- body:
  - h2 "What drives MOQ" → "Battery MOQ is usually tied to a container load, because shipping cost per unit collapses at FCL. Chengguang Energy's reference MOQ is one 20ft container (approximately 600–800 units depending on model)."
  - h2 "What drives lead time" → "Lead time covers order confirmation, production and pre-shipment testing. Chengguang Energy's reference lead time is 30–45 days from order confirmation; custom-label orders add sampling time."
  - h2 "How to reduce both" → bullet: "Order in full container multiples", "Consolidate mixed models into one container", "Confirm specification early to avoid sampling delays", "Plan around the harvest-free, year-round production of lead-acid"
- internal: link to /contact/ and /knowledge/how-oem-battery-programs-work/

## 4. slug: how-oem-battery-programs-work
- title: "How OEM Battery Programs Work | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "How an OEM battery program runs end to end — specification, sampling, production and repeat orders — for importers and brand owners."
- articleTitle: "How OEM battery programs work"
- lead: "An OEM battery program is a repeatable supply arrangement, not a one-off order. It runs in four phases."
- body:
  - h2 "1. Specification" → "You define the battery: standard, model, capacity, CCA, terminal, label and packaging. The manufacturer confirms it against their production capability."
  - h2 "2. Sampling" → "The manufacturer produces pre-production samples for your testing and approval before mass production."
  - h2 "3. Production" → "Approved specification goes to the production line. Chengguang Energy runs 18 lines with IATF 16949 quality control and 100% end-of-line testing."
  - h2 "4. Repeat orders" → "The specification is locked so subsequent orders match the approved sample. Repeatability is the point of an OEM program."
- internal: link to /oem/ and /knowledge/oem-battery-development-process/

## 5. slug: oem-battery-development-process
- title: "The OEM Battery Development Process | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "The step-by-step OEM battery development process — from requirement to shipment — and where buyers should be involved."
- articleTitle: "The OEM battery development process"
- lead: "OEM development is a gated process. Understanding the stages tells you where to be involved and where to insist on sign-off."
- body:
  - h2 "The seven stages" → ordered list: "1. Consultation", "2. Specification", "3. Design & branding", "4. Sampling", "5. Mass production", "6. Quality inspection", "7. Logistics & shipping"
  - h2 "Where buyers should be involved" → bullet: "Specification — sign off every parameter", "Sampling — test before approving", "Quality inspection — agree the acceptance criteria", "Shipment — confirm documentation"
  - h2 "Why gates matter" → "Each gate is a decision point. Skipping a gate is how a specification drifts and a repeat order comes back different."
- internal: link to /oem/ and /contact/

## 6. slug: how-build-private-label-battery-brand
- title: "How to Build a Private-Label Battery Brand | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "How to launch a private-label battery brand: positioning, specification, branding, packaging, certification and go-to-market."
- articleTitle: "How to build a private-label battery brand"
- lead: "A private-label battery brand starts with a positioning decision, not a label design. Here is the sequence."
- body:
  - h2 "1. Pick the position" → "Decide the market and segment: passenger car, truck, heavy-duty, start-stop. The position determines the standard (JIS, DIN, BCI) and the specification."
  - h2 "2. Fix the specification" → "Lock voltage, capacity, CCA, case size and terminal against your reference model. Confirm the standard family for your target market."
  - h2 "3. Brand it" → bullet: "Label artwork", "Packaging design", "Barcode and SKU planning", "Warranty terms"
  - h2 "4. Launch it" → "Order a container, plan the logistics, and put the documentation set in place (COA, traceability, test reports)."
- internal: link to /private-label/ and /knowledge/private-label-battery-strategy/

## 7. slug: private-label-battery-strategy
- title: "Private-Label Battery Strategy | Dingwei Battery"
- description: "A private-label battery strategy framework: positioning, differentiation, specification discipline and repeatable supply."
- articleTitle: "Private-label battery strategy"
- lead: "Private label is not just putting your name on a battery — it is a supply and positioning strategy."
