---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>OEM vs ODM vs private label</h1>
    <p class="lead">All three models let you sell batteries under your own brand. The difference is how much of the specification and design you control.</p>

    <h2>Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OEM</strong> — you define the specification and branding; the manufacturer builds to your requirements.</li>
      <li><strong>ODM</strong> — the manufacturer owns a ready design; you select it, adapt branding and optionally request changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Private label</strong> — a supplied product carries your name, label and packaging.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Which one fits your sourcing goal</h2>
    <div class="grid grid--3">
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose OEM</h3><p>When you have a defined technical specification and need exact control.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose ODM</h3><p>When you want a proven platform and faster time to market.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose private label</h3><p>When the priority is brand identity on an existing product.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2>What stays constant</h2>
    <p>Whichever path you choose, verify the standard family, voltage, capacity, CCA, dimensions, terminal layout, certifications and export terms before ordering.</p>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">Use the supplier checklist &rarr;</a> · <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-specification-guide/">Build a clean specification &rarr;</a></p>

